1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,425
Every time you open a web page,

2
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:04,225
your browser asks a question,

3
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:06,025
and a server gives an answer.

4
00:00:06,575 --> 00:00:08,769
That conversation has strict rules.

5
00:00:08,769 --> 00:00:09,959
A request goes out.

6
00:00:09,959 --> 00:00:11,338
A response comes back,

7
00:00:11,338 --> 00:00:13,594
with a number that says how it went.

8
00:00:14,144 --> 00:00:16,819
Today you learn those rules: methods,

9
00:00:16,819 --> 00:00:17,758
status codes,

10
00:00:17,758 --> 00:00:18,337
headers,

11
00:00:18,337 --> 00:00:21,589
and how HTTPS keeps the conversation private.

12
00:00:22,139 --> 00:00:27,089
You will send real requests, read real answers, and inspect a real certificate.

13
00:00:29,139 --> 00:00:31,464
First, the shape of a web conversation.

14
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:35,726
The client sends a request. It has a method, a path, and headers.

15
00:00:36,276 --> 00:00:40,543
The server sends a response. It has a status code, headers, and a body.

16
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:44,122
The body is the page, or the data, that you asked for.

17
00:00:44,672 --> 00:00:49,280
Everything in web work, from browsers to API calls, is this same exchange.

18
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:53,079
The method says what you want to do.

19
00:00:53,629 --> 00:00:56,509
GET: read something. It should never change anything.

20
00:00:57,059 --> 00:00:59,769
POST: send new data, like submitting a form.

21
00:01:00,319 --> 00:01:02,708
PUT: replace something that already exists.

22
00:01:03,258 --> 00:01:04,751
DELETE: remove something.

23
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:09,653
Safe reading is GET. Anything that changes data uses another method.

24
00:01:11,703 --> 00:01:17,335
Let us send a real request to a small web server on this computer.

25
00:01:17,335 --> 00:01:19,724
Type: curl -i localhost:8080

26
00:01:19,854 --> 00:01:25,572
The first line is the status line. H T T P one point one, status two hundred, O K.

27
00:01:26,122 --> 00:01:30,516
Then the headers: the server name, the date, and the type and size of the body.

28
00:01:31,066 --> 00:01:34,416
And below, a blank line, then the body: hello from the lab.

29
00:01:34,966 --> 00:01:45,099
Now a POST that sends data. Type: curl -s -X POST -d name=ana localhost:8080/form

30
00:01:45,229 --> 00:01:50,712
The server answers: created name equals ana. The data went in the body of the request.

31
00:01:52,762 --> 00:01:54,973
The status code tells you how it went.

32
00:01:54,973 --> 00:01:56,544
It is a three-digit number,

33
00:01:56,544 --> 00:01:58,522
and the first digit is the family.

34
00:01:59,072 --> 00:02:02,357
Two hundred family: success. Two hundred is O K.

35
00:02:02,907 --> 00:02:07,473
Three hundred family: go somewhere else. Three oh one means moved permanently.

36
00:02:08,023 --> 00:02:10,746
Four hundred family: your request was wrong.

37
00:02:10,746 --> 00:02:11,550
Four oh four,

38
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:12,169
not found.

39
00:02:12,169 --> 00:02:13,036
Four oh three,

40
00:02:13,036 --> 00:02:13,655
forbidden.

41
00:02:14,205 --> 00:02:18,621
Five hundred family: the server failed. Five hundred is a server error.

42
00:02:19,171 --> 00:02:21,240
Remember: four is you, five is them.

43
00:02:23,290 --> 00:02:32,975
Let us ask for a page that does not exist. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080/missing | head -1

44
00:02:33,105 --> 00:02:37,180
Four oh four, not found. The server is fine. The request was wrong.

45
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:50,210
Now a page that has moved. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080/old | grep -i "^HTTP\|^loc"

46
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:54,927
Three oh one, with a Location header. It tells the browser where to go next.

47
00:02:55,477 --> 00:03:03,711
And a server that fails. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080/boom | head -1

48
00:03:03,841 --> 00:03:07,233
Five hundred. This time the problem is on the server side.

49
00:03:09,283 --> 00:03:13,699
Headers are small labelled notes that travel with every request and response.

50
00:03:14,249 --> 00:03:18,345
Content-Type says what the body is: text, a web page, or data.

51
00:03:18,895 --> 00:03:21,199
Content-Length says how big the body is.

52
00:03:21,749 --> 00:03:24,629
Location says where to go next, after a redirect.

53
00:03:25,179 --> 00:03:29,552
Authorization carries your proof of identity, and Cookie carries your session.

54
00:03:30,102 --> 00:03:32,982
When something fails, the headers often tell you why.

55
00:03:35,032 --> 00:03:37,080
Plain H T T P has a problem.

56
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:41,662
Anyone on the path between you and the server can read it, or change it.

57
00:03:42,212 --> 00:03:47,865
H T T P S adds a lock. It runs the same conversation inside T L S.

58
00:03:48,415 --> 00:03:53,386
T L S does two jobs. It encrypts the traffic, and it proves who the server is.

59
00:03:53,936 --> 00:03:59,269
The padlock in your browser means the connection is encrypted and the server was verified.

60
00:03:59,269 --> 00:04:01,403
It does not mean the site is honest.

61
00:04:03,453 --> 00:04:06,376
How does the server prove who it is? With a certificate.

62
00:04:06,926 --> 00:04:10,318
A certificate says: this public key belongs to this name.

63
00:04:10,868 --> 00:04:14,964
It is signed by a certificate authority that your computer already trusts.

64
00:04:15,514 --> 00:04:17,631
Your computer checks three things.

65
00:04:17,631 --> 00:04:18,690
The name matches.

66
00:04:18,690 --> 00:04:20,247
The signature is trusted.

67
00:04:20,247 --> 00:04:21,679
And it has not expired.

68
00:04:22,229 --> 00:04:26,048
If any check fails, the connection is refused. That is a good thing.

69
00:04:28,098 --> 00:04:32,212
Now a secure server on this computer,

70
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:36,327
with a certificate we made ourselves.

71
00:04:36,327 --> 00:04:42,221
Type: curl -sSI https://localhost:8443 2>&1 | head -1

72
00:04:42,351 --> 00:04:44,683
Certificate problem: self-signed.

73
00:04:44,683 --> 00:04:46,873
No trusted authority signed it,

74
00:04:46,873 --> 00:04:48,004
so curl refuses.

75
00:04:48,004 --> 00:04:49,135
Exit code sixty.

76
00:04:49,685 --> 00:04:52,384
To look at the page anyway,

77
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:56,383
we can switch the check off with dash k.

78
00:04:56,383 --> 00:04:58,582
Only do that in a lab.

79
00:04:58,582 --> 00:05:03,381
Type: curl -skI https://localhost:8443 | head -1

80
00:05:03,511 --> 00:05:07,436
Two hundred, O K. The connection works, but we skipped the safety check.

81
00:05:07,986 --> 00:05:11,733
Now read the certificate itself.

82
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:18,525
Type: openssl x509 -in tls/cert.pem -noout -subject -dates

83
00:05:18,655 --> 00:05:21,134
The subject is the name it vouches for.

84
00:05:21,134 --> 00:05:22,787
And two dates: not before,

85
00:05:22,787 --> 00:05:23,677
and not after.

86
00:05:23,677 --> 00:05:25,012
That is its lifetime.

87
00:05:27,062 --> 00:05:28,705
Match the symptom to the cause.

88
00:05:29,255 --> 00:05:32,391
Four oh four: wrong path. Check the address you typed.

89
00:05:32,941 --> 00:05:36,119
Five hundred: the server broke. Read the server logs.

90
00:05:36,669 --> 00:05:40,509
Certificate problem: the name, the date, or the signer is wrong.

91
00:05:41,059 --> 00:05:44,046
Too many redirects: two rules send you back and forth.

92
00:05:44,596 --> 00:05:48,661
Connection refused or timed out: that is not H T T P at all.

93
00:05:48,661 --> 00:05:50,761
Go back to ports and firewalls.

94
00:05:52,811 --> 00:05:54,240
Watch out for four myths.

95
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:59,548
Myth one: the padlock means the site is safe. It means the connection is private.

96
00:06:00,098 --> 00:06:05,196
Myth two: a four hundred error is the server's fault. Four hundred is the request's fault.

97
00:06:05,746 --> 00:06:09,672
Myth three: dash k fixes certificate errors. It only hides them.

98
00:06:10,222 --> 00:06:14,979
Myth four: GET and POST are just two names for the same thing. They have different rules.

99
00:06:15,529 --> 00:06:17,193
Read the status code first.

100
00:06:19,243 --> 00:06:21,568
Here is how you check your own understanding.

101
00:06:22,118 --> 00:06:24,572
Name the parts of a request and a response.

102
00:06:25,122 --> 00:06:26,978
Choose the right method for a job.

103
00:06:27,528 --> 00:06:29,405
Say what each status family means.

104
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:32,280
Read a status line and a header with curl.

105
00:06:32,830 --> 00:06:35,966
Explain what H T T P S and a certificate add.

106
00:06:36,516 --> 00:06:38,607
Explain why a certificate can fail.

107
00:06:39,157 --> 00:06:40,778
To pass, you need all six.

108
00:06:42,828 --> 00:06:45,111
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

109
00:06:45,661 --> 00:06:51,719
One: pick any public site, and run curl -I on its address. Read the status line.

110
00:06:52,269 --> 00:06:55,896
Two: add a path that does not exist, and find the four oh four.

111
00:06:56,446 --> 00:07:02,825
Three: run curl -v on an H T T P S address, and find the line about the certificate.

112
00:07:03,375 --> 00:07:07,727
Four: use curl with dash L, to follow a redirect, and see where it ends.

113
00:07:08,277 --> 00:07:14,058
Five: find the expiry date with openssl s client, or in your browser's padlock.

114
00:07:14,608 --> 00:07:17,915
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

115
00:07:33,465 --> 00:07:34,340
Quick check.

116
00:07:34,890 --> 00:07:37,279
What are the parts of a request and a response?

117
00:07:37,829 --> 00:07:39,578
What does each status family mean?

118
00:07:40,128 --> 00:07:42,091
What two jobs does T L S do?

119
00:07:42,641 --> 00:07:44,476
Why would a certificate be refused?

120
00:07:45,026 --> 00:07:49,228
Objective covered: MC03-O03.

121
00:07:49,778 --> 00:07:51,848
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

122
00:08:02,398 --> 00:08:06,067
Next: names, D N S, and a request from start to finish.

123
00:08:06,617 --> 00:08:08,494
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
